  "comment": "**What it does**\nGood businesses that are **temporarily cheap** - value WITH proof of life, so you skip the traps that deserve to be cheap.\n\n- **Cheap two ways** - a 15%+ margin of safety against fair value, AND cheaper than its own industry on P/E (falling back to P/S).\n- **Still cheap in the bear case** - `margin_of_safety_bear > 0` re-runs the valuation on pessimistic growth. This is the strongest single value-trap test you have.\n- **Good** - ROIC 10%+, profitable, current ratio 1.1+.\n- **Proof of life** - back above the 200-day average, so the market has stopped voting against it.\n- **Trap guards** - at most 1 bear flag (weak cash conversion, thin moat, weak interest cover, earnings-quality risk), and no valuation-guardrail warning.\n\n**Why 15% and not the 30% you originally wrote**\nThe valuation engine now fades growth down to the terminal rate, which lowered fair values across the board - so 30% today means something stricter than it did when you wrote the brief. **15% is the post-fade equivalent of 'meaningfully cheap'.**\n\nIn practice the number barely matters here: **30% gives 22 names, 15% gives 25, 0% gives 27.** The bear-case test is already doing the work, exactly as your update predicted.\n\n**How to tweak**\n- **Do not reach for the margin-of-safety number to resize this list** - it is nearly flat. The blocks that actually bind are `roic` (176 to 77) and the MA200 proof-of-life test (55 to 32).\n- **Want more names?** Drop `roic` to 8, or relax the MA200 test to catch names earlier in their recovery (at the cost of more falling knives).\n- **Want stricter?** Require `bear_flag_count = 0`.\n\n**How to sort for best picks**\n- **Sort by `margin_of_safety_bear`** - still cheap in the pessimistic case is the strongest single value test. Or use `value_score` for the blended view.\n- **Tiebreak on `quality_score`** - among equally cheap names, the better business wins. `bear_flag_count` ascending works as a second tiebreak.\n- **Avoid `momentum_score`** - genuine value usually means out of favour, so ranking on momentum fights the thesis.",
  "ai_instructions": "Find good businesses that are temporarily cheap \u2014 value WITH proof of life, so I\nskip the value traps (cheap companies that deserve to be cheap).\n- **Goal**: value. **Dividends**: ignore.\n- **Cheap**: trading well below fair value (margin of safety ~30%+) AND cheaper\n  than its own industry.\n- **Good**: real profitability (ROIC ~10%+) and passing basic health checks \u2014\n  profitable companies only.\n- **Proof of life**: price at least back above its 200-day average, so the market\n  has stopped voting against it.\n- **Size**: $1B and bigger. **Kinds**: normal stocks only. **Risk**: moderate.\n- **Results**: ~20-150 names. **Sorting**: value first, tiebreak quality.\n\nUpdate (2026-08-13) \u2014 the valuation engine changed underneath this filter, so the\nold numbers don't mean the same thing:\n- **`margin_of_safety` is now a stricter bar than it was.** The DCF/DDM behind\n  `fair_value` now fade growth down to the terminal rate instead of holding the\n  trailing rate flat for ten years, which lowered fair value for most ordinary\n  companies. A 30% margin of safety is therefore harder to clear than when this\n  brief was written \u2014 **recalibrate the threshold against a dry run** rather than\n  assuming 30% still returns a similar-sized list. Lower it if the list comes back\n  too thin; the intent is \"meaningfully cheap\", not the specific number.\n- **Use the bear case for the value-trap half of the goal.** There are now\n  `margin_of_safety_bear` / `fair_value_bear` columns (the same models re-run on\n  pessimistic growth). \"Still undervalued in the bear case\" is a much better\n  value-trap filter than the base number alone \u2014 prefer requiring a positive\n  `margin_of_safety_bear` over pushing the base threshold higher.\n- **`bear_flag_count` is built for exactly this brief's \"deserve to be cheap\"\n  concern** \u2014 it counts red flags (weak cash conversion, thin moat, weak interest\n  coverage, earnings-quality risk). Requiring a low count (0, or at most 1) is a\n  cheap way to screen out the traps. `valuation_guardrail_flag` marks names whose\n  valuation assumptions are at the edge of plausibility \u2014 worth excluding here.",
